/* ============================================================
   Cigar Cafe — 上級編の英語本文 2/4（キューバ学）
   英語版でだけ読み込まれ、ADVANCED_DATA の該当項目を英語文に置き換える。
   日本語版はこのファイルを読まない。元データ: data/advanced.js, data/advanced_deep.js
   ============================================================ */
(function () {
  if (typeof ADVANCED_DATA === "undefined") return;
  var A = ADVANCED_DATA;

  A.vitolaNaming = "【The double naming system】A Cuban cigar carries two names for a single size: the factory standard name used inside the rolling hall, the 'vitola de galera', and the commercial name the consumer sees on box and band, the 'vitola de salida'. Learning to tell these two layers apart puts you at the threshold of connoisseurship.\n【The origin of 'galera'】The 'galera' is the hall where cigars are rolled, and the name is said to derive from a likeness once drawn to a galley ship, its convict oarsmen ranged along long benches. It is factory-floor language, born of the sight of the rollers (torcedores) seated in rows at their tables.\n【Defining the de galera】The vitola de galera is an internal standard fixing only length and thickness (ring gauge), and it is shared across marques. The Laguito No.1 (192mm × 38), for example, is used by several brands: it is a pure dimensional name, indifferent to differences of taste or blend.\n【Defining the de salida】The vitola de salida is the market name a cigar receives once it has 'left' (salida) the factory, and it is what appears on the box and the band. The same Laguito No.1 becomes the 'Lanceros' at Cohiba and takes another name at Trinidad — blend and product name are where individuality parts ways.\n【A standard that never reaches the box】As a rule the de galera name is never printed on the box: it is an internal cipher the consumer has no need to know. The official Habanos glossary sets out this two-layer structure explicitly, and it helps to think of the galera names as the language of the rollers and of production management.\n【Numbers alongside names】Each galera may also carry an identifying number (609, for instance, for the Churchill-shaped Julieta No.2). In the factory these numbers and dimensions standardise production, managing the same shape across marques from a single register.\n【Thinking in three layers】The connoisseur holds three layers together: 'Cohiba Lanceros (salida) = Laguito No.1 (galera) = the so-called lancero shape (the English-speaking nickname)'. Once this correspondence is in your head, you can place the size and lineage of an unfamiliar marque at a glance.\n【Reading taste from dimensions】Know the galeras and you can predict character from the dimensions. Short, thick shapes (the robusto and its kin) burn slowly and taste rich; long, slender shapes (the lancero and its kin) are delicate and aromatic, yet count as showpieces precisely because rolling them evenly demands such skill.\n【Derivative English nicknames】English-speaking terms such as Churchill, robusto and double corona are trade nicknames derived from these Cuban standards and product names, and often carry no strict dimensional definition. The original point of reference remains, throughout, the de galera.\n【Fixing it with examples】The galera of the Trinidad Fundadores is the 'Laguito Especial' (192×40); that of the Cohiba Siglo I is the 'Perla' (about 102×40). Typical cases of the salida name changing with the marque even within the same slender family — and knowing the correspondences raises the resolution of both conversation and buying by a notch.\n【Reading the ring gauge】Thickness is expressed as a ring gauge in units of 1/64 of an inch: 38 means 38/64 inch, 50 means 50/64 inch, and the number ending a galera name corresponds to this. In a notation such as '192×38', the right-hand figure is the ring gauge and the left the length in millimetres.\n【The figurado lineage】Shapes tapering at the head or at both ends — pirámide, belicoso, salomones — are the figurados, harder to roll than a uniform cylinder. The tapered head is held to narrow the smoke's path and concentrate the flavour, and the galera names let you identify this lineage.\n【The robusto revival】The modern protagonist, the robusto (124×50), is a comparatively recent taste, established since the revival of thick gauges in the 1970s. Before that the long, slender corona family was the classical yardstick; history falls into place once you see that the short, thick shape's rise to stardom is a modern development.\n【Same galera, different taste】Even within the same robusto galera, the Partagás Serie D No.4 and the Cohiba Robustos differ in leaf blend and ageing, and taste like different cigars. The crucial point is that the galera is a common language of dimensions only — it guarantees nothing about commonality of taste.\n【The numbering system】Galera names are often tied to unique numbers, and the factories draw up cross-marque production plans by these numbers. The consumer only ever touches the product name (the salida), but once you know the number-and-dimension bookkeeping behind the scenes, it makes sense that the same shape keeps surfacing under different marques.\n【Putting it to use】Enthusiasts use the galera as a measuring rod for choosing. Anchor yourself on a favourite galera known from experience (the robusto, say, or the corona gorda), and even with an unfamiliar marque you can pick that same galera size and have a fair estimate of smoking time and intensity — a practical way of avoiding disappointments.";

  var GAL = [
    { dim: "192×38", ex: "Cohiba Lanceros and part of the Trinidad range. The archetype of the slender, elongated 'lancero' shape, a showpiece notoriously hard to roll evenly. The narrow gauge makes lighting and burn control difficult; delicacy and high aroma are its calling cards. It is also the format that symbolises Cohiba's founding years" },
    { dim: "178×47", ex: "Romeo y Julieta Churchill, Cohiba Esplendidos. The classic large format known in English as the 'Churchill', an unhurried smoke of around two hours. A cigar for company, uniting stature and balance" },
    { dim: "194×49", ex: "Partagás Lusitanias. Known in English as the 'double corona'. A commanding length of over 19cm, offering a long arc of flavour from opening to close. One takes an hour and a half or more — a 'sit-down cigar' for following the evolution of taste at leisure. A representative large format demanding seasoned rolling" },
    { dim: "156×52", ex: "Montecristo No.2. The tapered-head 'pirámide/torpedo' shape, whose point concentrates the smoke and focuses the flavour. Richness in fine proportion — sometimes called the king of the figurados. A point for the connoisseur: where you cut the head fine-tunes the aperture, letting you tailor the draw and taste to your own liking" },
    { dim: "140×52", ex: "H. Upmann No.2, Bolívar Belicosos. The 'belicoso' shape, shorter in the body than the pirámide, prized for the concentration and power that rise from its stubby tapered head" },
    { dim: "143×46", ex: "Cohiba Siglo IV, the Ramón Allones family. A middle-of-the-road size of about 5⅝ × 46 with a fine balance of flavour and smoking time — an all-round format that easily becomes a daily mainstay. Smokeable in about an hour, it unites handiness with satisfaction, and its popularity has grown in recent years" },
    { dim: "165×42", ex: "Montecristo No.1. Known in English as the 'lonsdale'. A slender classic size in which the quality of the leaf shows through honestly, giving an elegant, linear build of flavour. With girth held back, the leaf's individuality and aroma rise candidly — a classic form favoured by those who want the wrapper's aromas to lead" },
    { dim: "142×42", ex: "Montecristo No.3. Known in English as the 'corona'. The classic of classics, held to be the yardstick for all dimensions — a middle format for gauging a marque's unadorned taste. With length and girth both moderate, the blend shows itself most honestly, and connoisseurs prize it as a reference instrument for comparing marques" },
    { dim: "129×42", ex: "Montecristo No.4. Known in English as the 'petit corona'. Said to be Cuba's most-produced staple size — an introduction and a mainstay at once, giving a marque's character in a short sitting. Finished in around 40–45 minutes, it suits everyday smoking and side-by-side tasting of several marques, and is an easy entry on cost as well" },
    { dim: "124×50", ex: "Partagás Serie D No.4, Cohiba Robustos. The 'robusto', about 4⅞ × 50. Thick, short and rich — the most popular shape of the present day. A modern protagonist revived in the 1970s, its worldwide following owes much to the efficiency of tasting the leaf's savour in concentrated form in about 45 minutes to an hour" },
    { dim: "150×52", ex: "Cohiba Siglo VI. A thickish robusto extra, the summit size of Cohiba's Línea 1492, offering concentrated sweet aromas and smoothness to be savoured at length" },
    { dim: "164×54", ex: "Cohiba Sublimes (first seen as an EL), Montecristo Sublimes. A volume-class format uniting a 54 ring gauge with length — a modern large format popular in limited editions" },
    { dim: "119×52 / 144×54 / 166×56", ex: "The three special galeras reserved for Cohiba Behike. Using the rare medio tiempo leaf; at their announcement in 2010 all were set as new dimensions without precedent at Habanos — the summit of the range" },
    { dim: "102×40", ex: "Cohiba Siglo I. A small size of about 4 × 40 in the petit corona class, delivering quality in a short sitting. Thin and short, it is also a size in which flaws of rolling and leaf show readily" },
    { dim: "192×40", ex: "Trinidad Fundadores. A slender shape fractionally thicker than the Laguito No.1, recognisable by its pigtail (the plaited head). A standard symbolising the lineage of the elegant diplomatic cigar" },
    { dim: "127×48", ex: "Hoyo de Monterrey Epicure No.2, the Cohiba Siglo II family. An easy-handling medium-thick size of about 5 × 48, a popular format suited to gentle, refined medium bodies. A touch longer and slimmer than the robusto, this graceful standard is much used for marques seeking smoothness and delicacy of aroma rather than power" },
    { dim: "140×52", ex: "Cohiba Maduro 5 Genios. A thickish robusto class of about 5½ × 52, used for the line of maduro leaf aged five years or more, drawing out a sweet, smooth richness" },
    { dim: "184×57", ex: "Partagás Salomones (for LCDH and the like). A stately large figurado tapered at both ends, with an extreme 57 ring gauge. An ostentatious outsize demanding great rolling skill and quantities of leaf. A monumental format taking several hours for a single cigar — a rarity often to be met only through limited channels such as La Casa del Habano" },
    { dim: "102×50", ex: "The various 'petit robusto' shapes. A short, thick modern small format of about 4 × 50, concentrating the richness of a robusto into a short sitting. Giving satisfaction in 20–30 minutes, it suits a smoke snatched between engagements — a nimble format whose popularity has grown in recent years" }
  ];
  if (A.galeraNames.length === GAL.length) A.galeraNames.forEach(function (o, i) { Object.assign(o, GAL[i]); });

  A.boxCodes = "【Two kinds of stamp】The underside of a Cuban box carries two main stamps: the 'factory code' (cuño), showing which factory rolled the cigars, and the 'date code', showing when they were packed. The two are stamped as a pair, and together they are the starting point for judging authenticity and vintage.\n【Origins of the factory code】The factory code is said to have been introduced in 1985, in the Cubatabaco era, under the then director Francisco Padrón. Before that there was only the 'Hecho en Cuba' stamp, and no cipher existed to identify the factory.\n【Encryption and frequent change】Since around 1998, Habanos S.A. (and Cubatabaco) have repeatedly encrypted and changed the codes, and no official key is published. The assignments change every few years, so even the experts find complete decipherment difficult.\n【Unofficial decipherment in practice】With no key available, the reality is that collector communities pool their boxes and decode cumulatively, sharing the results. Known past examples include El Laguito = LOME, H. Upmann = ECA, Partagás = OSU and Romeo y Julieta = PEL, but the assignments differ by vintage.\n【The current date code】Since 2000 the month and year of manufacture (packing) have been standardised as the three-letter Spanish month abbreviation plus the last two digits of the year: ENE (January), FEB (February), MAR, ABR, MAY, JUN, JUL, AGO, SEP, OCT, NOV and DIC (December) — so 'AGO19' = August 2019.\n【Before 2000】Before 2000 the date-code system changed several times, and there were periods when a different, encoded scheme was in use. Pinning down those vintages takes specialist references; it is safest to assume that the simple month-plus-year reading basically applies only from 2000 onwards.\n【The REVISADO stamp】Following quality problems stemming from the poor 1999 leaf harvest, some boxes from around 2000 carry an additional 'REVISADO' ('inspected') stamp. This is held to be a marker peculiar to that period, indicating a box passed through quality re-inspection, and it serves as supporting evidence for dating too.\n【Barcode serials】Since the introduction of the new warranty seal in 2009, boxes have carried a unique barcode-style serial, tracked in a central database from factory dispatch to each country's official distributor. It can be checked on the verification page of the official site, though users report the service running unreliably, so it should not be leaned on too heavily.\n【The weight of the wording】Alongside 'Hecho en Cuba' and 'Habanos s.a.', the underside carries wording indicating the hand-rolled grade. In 1989 the ambiguous 'Hecho a mano', which could include machine assistance, gave way to 'Totalmente a mano', declaring every stage done by hand — and the latter is the norm for genuine premium cigars.\n【The principle of combined judgement】A factory code absent from the current unofficial lists does not immediately mean a fake: the box may be old, or the code simply unrecorded. The working rule is to read factory code, date code, serial, wording and the quality of the stamping together, judging by their mutual consistency rather than by any one alone.\n【Checking a box in hand】With a box in hand, look first at whether the underside stamps are impressed as one and the typefaces match. Inconsistencies — one stamp oddly crisp, misaligned placement, the impression of having been added later — are warning signals to heed before any decoding of the factory code itself.\n【Codes on the cigars themselves】In recent years codes have sometimes appeared not only on boxes but on the cellophane or bands of individual cigars. Whether these agree with the box's information — whether date codes and brand wording run consistently throughout — is further material for cross-checking authenticity.\n【Date codes and ageing】The date code serves ageing management as well as authentication. Because even the same marque varies with harvest and production year, enthusiasts use the codes to pick boxes from 'good years' and as a guide to how long to rest a purchase. Being able to read the vintage feeds directly into the breadth of ways a cigar can be enjoyed.\n【The place of the origin marking】The 'Hecho en Cuba' on the underside is the basic marking of origin, the counterpart of the 'Hecho en Rep. Dominicana' and similar stamps on non-Cuban boxes. Together with the company name 'Habanos s.a.', only when all of these are present are the outward conditions of a genuine Cuban Habano met — and checking in that order is the practical approach.\n【An irony of forgery】The encrypted factory code carries an irony: precisely because the key is unpublished, it is easy for forgers to reproduce. Stamping three plausible letters is no great feat, so the sensible attitude is to weight the code as 'points added if consistent, points off if clearly contradictory' — and never to let it stand alone as decisive proof.";

  A.brandHierarchy = "【Habanos S.A. and the DOP】Habanos S.A., founded in 1994, is the company with exclusive worldwide distribution of Cuban premium cigars, home to all 27 marques. These fall under the protected designation of origin (D.O.P. Habano, instituted in 1994), whose production standards are overseen by a dedicated regulatory council.\n【What the DOP means】The D.O.P. is a legal certification granted only to cigars made from leaf grown in specified Cuban districts (vegas) and passed through strict standards and quality control. Aimed at counterfeit prevention and quality assurance, it can be called the guarantee of 'made in Cuba', reserved for the 27 great marques.\n【A refreshed classification】In recent years Habanos S.A. has reorganised its marques into two broad groups, 'Global brands' and 'Portfolio (local) brands' — a change held to simplify the old finer-grained rankings and clarify each marque's place in distribution strategy.\n【The six Global brands】The Global tier comprises the six best-known flagship marques — Cohiba, Montecristo, Romeo y Julieta, Partagás, Hoyo de Monterrey and H. Upmann — carried in every market and in every La Casa del Habano.\n【Inside the Portfolio】The remaining marques fall into the Portfolio, branching into the connoisseur-orientated, small-circulation 'niche (specialty)' group, strongly regional staples, and more affordable volume marques. Bolívar, Punch, Ramón Allones, Trinidad, Cuaba and San Cristóbal are placed here (the naming of the categories and the assignment of individual marques vary between sources).\n【The niche marques】The niche group circulates worldwide but in limited quantities and without aggressive promotion — marques 'known only to those in the know'. Cuaba, San Cristóbal de La Habana and Trinidad are often cited as representative examples, though the wording of the classification shifts from year to year.\n【The layer of líneas】Within each marque, sub-series called 'líneas' divide taste, rank and price. Cohiba, for example: the Línea Clásica (traditional sizes), the Línea 1492 (Siglo I–VI, marking the 500th anniversary of Columbus's arrival), Behike (the summit of 2010) and Maduro 5 (leaf aged five years or more).\n【Cohiba and the factories】Cohiba, created in 1966, has the special pedigree of long being reserved for dignitaries and for Castro's gifts, and is rolled at the storied El Laguito factory. Montecristo, born at the H. Upmann factory in 1935, is the staple of staples, sometimes called the benchmark of all Habanos.\n【The historical background】With the nationalisation of the industry in 1961 the brands passed under state control, and were later consolidated in Habanos S.A. That the former owners' exile became a distant cause of the parallel existence of non-Cuban versions (the CC-versus-NC question, treated later) is also worth holding in mind as background to this hierarchy.\n【The frame of 27 marques】The current Habanos S.A. line-up comprises roughly 27 marques, and the DOP is held to cover this range. Marques fall dormant and revive in the course of production consolidation, so the count fluctuates slightly with time, but the skeleton of the great names has long been maintained.\n【Ties to the factories】Each marque is rolled at particular factories and lines: Cohiba at El Laguito, Montecristo historically in the H. Upmann factory line — and these pairings too are clues to a brand's rank and character.\n【The hierarchy of price】Rank is reflected in price. Global flagships and top lines such as Behike occupy the high price band, while volume marques like José L. Piedra sit in the affordable range; the spread even within Cuban production is very wide, and understanding the hierarchy also guides how a budget should be allocated.\n【Character among the niche marques】Trinidad has an elegant style originating in Castro's diplomatic cigars; Cuaba has the pointed individuality of specialising in tapered figurados; San Cristóbal de La Habana is a comparatively young marque naming its cigars after Havana's fortresses and quarters. Each niche marque has a clear character of its own, to spur the connoisseur's curiosity.\n【The uniformity the DOP secures】The DOP's regulatory council imposes standards across the whole process, from cultivation on the farm through fermentation, rolling and boxing, and polices quality with sampling inspections. By this mechanism even a volume marque is — at least in principle — guaranteed the baseline quality of being genuinely Cuban, and being able to expect a certain standard even at the lower tiers is held to be a strength of Habanos.";

  var SPE = [
    { desc: "The annual limited edition begun in 2000. The requirements: a size absent from the regular range, rolled from leaf aged at least two years. The wrapper is usually dark (towards maduro), and a second band, black lettering on gold, is the marker. In 2002 the release is said to have been forgone, owing among other things to a shortage of suitable two-year-aged leaf, and secondary-market value varies greatly with year and marque. Each year's announcement is a major event in the cigar world — which marque will appear, and in which size, is keenly watched — and in favoured years an EL running scarce and dear straight after release is nothing unusual." },
    { desc: "Begun around 2005. Limited editions for the official distributors of particular countries or regions, made as a rule from local/Portfolio marques. They carry a second band reading 'Exclusivo' plus the region's name, and for a set period after release are sold only in that region. Small production runs make them hard to obtain elsewhere, and they readily become objects of speculation and collecting." },
    { desc: "Begun in 2003. A programme rolling regular sizes solely from selected leaf aged at least three years. Marked out by a dedicated silver-and-black band and a serially numbered lacquered box, in the premium price band. Its selling point is the rounded, settled taste of ageing traceable to a specific harvest year (cosecha). The mellow smoothness absent from young regular production, and the transparency of declaring the cosecha, earn it high regard from lovers of aged tobacco." },
    { desc: "The senior version of the Reserva, begun in 2009 — the summit of the ageing programme, rolled from selected leaf aged at least five years. Dressed in a gold-and-black band and a numbered de-luxe box, it forms the very top price band. Rare leaf from a single harvest year is used, and production is held to a very small number. The extraordinarily smooth, deep taste born of five-year-aged leaf is regarded as the crystallisation of Habanos' skill and patience, and as a prize coveted by collectors it often changes hands well above list price." },
    { desc: "Special editions marking anniversaries of a marque's founding or of a factory. With dedicated bands and special boxes, their commemorative nature and small numbers tend to command high prices on the secondary market. They appear irregularly, timed to milestones, and become the centrepieces of collections. The objects commemorated range widely across marques, factories and the company itself — Cohiba's 40th and 50th, Montecristo's 80th, humidor projects celebrating Habanos S.A.'s own milestones — and they draw the connoisseur's eye also for frequently involving aged leaf or special blends." },
    { desc: "Sizes and series sold exclusively through La Casa del Habano, the official Habanos specialist chain of more than 140 shops across five continents. Special network-only sizes such as the Partagás Salomones, and the Colección Habanos among others, can be had only here." },
    { desc: "The annual exclusive editions for member shops of the Tobacconists' Association of America, the US cigar retailers' body. Small in circulation and obtainable only through member shops, hence popular with collectors. The TAA is an industry association gathering leading American retailers; TAA exclusives exist on both the Cuban and the non-Cuban side, but the Cuban TAA releases mostly present existing sizes in special combinations or boxes, and rarity and standing vary from release to release." },
    { desc: "The 'aged' (Añejados) series announced at the end of 2014. Where the EL ages the leaf before rolling, the fundamental difference here is that the finished cigars are rested in their boxes for five to eight years before shipping. Existing regular sizes are used, with a dedicated band declaring the ageing. Resting them box and all lets the whole cigar — filler and wrapper alike — mellow as one; but opinion divides on the direction of the ageing and the gain in complexity, with critical voices finding the change less dramatic than hoped." },
    { desc: "Sumptuous collection boxes made for limited channels such as La Casa del Habano. Many have elaborate designs — cases bound to resemble books — and their numbered small runs are held to give them high collecting value. Alongside commemorative packaging projects such as replica humidors, they are a centrepiece for enthusiasts. The contents often include special sizes or limited blends; the weight of the concept lies less on smoking than on the pleasure of display and possession, and there is a constituency that keeps them unopened as investments." }
  ];
  if (A.specialEditions.length === SPE.length) A.specialEditions.forEach(function (o, i) { Object.assign(o, SPE[i]); });

  var SPF = [
    { p: "A history of the warranty seal",
      h: "Habanos S.A. (founded 1994) has from the outset affixed the green certificate-of-origin seal (Denominación de Origen) to every box. The prototype was introduced by decree of the Spanish king in 1889; in 1912 the government of independent Cuba enacted a design close to the present one; a minor revision followed in 1931; and in 1999 a red serial number and a hidden emblem visible only under ultraviolet (UV) light were added. Then in 2009 came a new seal with greatly strengthened anti-counterfeiting features — a two-colour hologram on the right, a unique barcode on the left differing box by box, a UV-fluorescent watermark, and a special material (synthetic auto-destructive paper) that destroys itself if forcibly peeled. This 2009 type circulated in parallel with the old one for several years; during the transition there are said to have been boxes without holograms and boxes with double-applied seals, so an old design does not of itself condemn a box as fake. The design has continued to be revised since, by marque and line, and the approach Habanos S.A. itself recommends is to judge by several features layered together rather than by the seal's match alone." },
    { p: "The Cohiba band hologram — the 'small head within the head'",
      h: "Within the industry, Cohiba is held to be the most imitated marque in the world. According to reports in specialist media such as Cigar Aficionado, the hologram style originating with Behike spread to the bands of the entire Cohiba range, engraved with a double structure in which a smaller head floats within the Taíno head image (head-within-head). The black-and-white chequerboard at the top of the band is reported to have gained additional rows, with a gold line added along the upper edge. The Behike line carried a dedicated engraved hologram band from its launch in 2010, and was later reported updated to a new hologram with enhanced traceability and security detection. Coarse printing, a flat single-layer hologram without depth, a smudged head image — all are signs to arouse suspicion; but as details of the design differ by year and line, it is important not to leap from 'one point of difference from my genuine example' straight to 'fake'." },
    { p: "Barcode serials and official verification",
      h: "Since the 2009-type seal, boxes have carried a unique barcode-style serial which, according to Habanos S.A., is tracked in a central database, tied to destination and invoice data, from factory dispatch until arrival at the official distributor in each country. The serial can be checked on the 'Authenticity Check' page of the official site (habanos.com), but user reports say the tool is repeatedly under maintenance or temporarily down, and it cannot be said to function stably at all times. In recent years some ultra-premium lines such as Behike are reported to have gained embedded NFC chips allowing direct verification by smartphone. Failure to verify does not immediately prove a fake; the practical course is to treat verification as one piece of evidence among several." },
    { p: "Factory codes and date codes — the limits of unofficial decipherment",
      h: "The roughly three-letter factory code on the underside is said to have been introduced in 1985, in the Cubatabaco era, under the then director Francisco Padrón; before that there was only the 'Hecho en Cuba' stamp. Habanos S.A. regards the code strictly as an internal mark for production management and fault-tracing, and publishes no key. Hence the reality is that collector communities pool their boxes and decode cumulatively, and judgement always carries uncertainty. A code absent from the circulating unofficial lists is not necessarily fake (codes change every few years, so it may simply be unrecorded); the factory code alone should remain a supporting clue, weighed together with other evidence." },
    { p: "The weight of 'Totalmente a mano'",
      h: "In 1989 Cuban hand-made cigars switched from the former wording 'Hecho a mano' to the stricter 'Totalmente a mano' ('totally by hand'). The former is an ambiguous phrase that could include machine-assisted binder work, whereas the latter is the declaration of the top grade: every stage, including the bunching of the long filler, done by hand. The underside carries this phrase alongside the 'Hecho en Cuba' and 'Habanos s.a.' stamps; since a genuine premium Habano lacking the phrase is essentially not to be expected, its presence and the quality of the stamping (depth of impression, uniformity of typeface) form an important item in authentication." },
    { p: "Forensics of band printing",
      h: "A genuine Habanos band is printed in many colours with extremely precise registration of the colour plates, and the gold-foil areas are stamped under even pressure so that the embossing (relief) is clearly felt with a finger. Line weights and letter spacing of the roman serif type are strictly controlled plate by plate. Counterfeits, by contrast, show colour bleed and misregistration; gold areas that are mere gold-coloured printing ink, without foil or embossing; irregular letterforms and spacing; and at times spelling errors (misspellings of 'Habana' and the like) have been reported. On the black-and-white chequerboard along the lower part of a Cohiba band, the squares on a genuine band run in good order to the very edge without truncation, while on counterfeits the pattern tends to break off raggedly or distort at the edges. Sophisticated forgeries are on the rise, however, and the recommended attitude is not to conclude from printing precision alone but to combine it with other evidence." },
    { p: "The triple cap and the precision of the finish",
      h: "Most genuine Cuban cigars are finished at the head (the mouth end) with the 'triple cap', a technique peculiar to hand-rolling that layers three small pieces of wrapper leaf. Hard to reproduce by machine, it yields a flat, well-proportioned head and a durability that resists unravelling. A head that has merely been rounded over, or is crudely glued and starting to peel, is grounds to suspect a fake — or at the least a laxly controlled example. Even genuine cigars occasionally vary in finish, however, so the practical course is to weigh this together with the density of the roll and the weight in the hand rather than on its own." },
    { p: "Box construction, materials, and the consistency of the stamps",
      h: "By a rule of thumb widely shared in the trade, no genuine Cuban box specification has a glass lid, and a glass-topped box is held to be judged a fake on that ground alone with next to no risk of error. A genuine regular Cohiba box is finished on the outside in glossy lacquer, and the quality of the wood and the precision of the hinges meet a consistent standard as an industrial product. Whether the stamps on the underside — factory code, date code, 'Totalmente a mano' and the rest — agree with one another in typeface, placement and depth of impression (one oddly crisp, or out of position) is another point to check. Labels, seals or stamps that look tacked onto the box after the fact are a strong warning sign." },
    { p: "Density of the roll, weight in the hand, and the colour of the ash",
      h: "Genuine cigars are rolled to high density from well-matured, good-quality leaf, so they feel remarkably weighty for their size, with a packed resilience that springs back under light finger pressure. Counterfeits using scrap or poor leaf are light and loosely filled, denting easily under a finger. The colour of the ash when smoking is another clue: Cuban ash traditionally tends towards grey or a mottled 'salt-and-pepper' (attributed to the minerals the leaf contains), while a pure white, uniform ash can in some cases suggest non-Cuban leaf or leaf raised under different fertilisation. Ash colour also varies greatly with soil, fertiliser and weather, however, and it should be borne in mind that on its own it is not conclusive evidence." },
    { p: "Street sellers in Havana and Cuba's tourist areas",
      h: "What several industry media and travel guides for visitors (Cigar Aficionado, the Havana Times, the Cayman Compass among them) repeatedly report is that street and tout selling of cigars at prices far below official retail is a settled fact of life in Havana and the island's tourist resorts. The typical patter is that 'a relative who works at the factory brought them out the back door' — in most cases held to be a stock sales line for making counterfeits believable. The empirical view of the industry press is that a considerable share of the 'bargains' pressed on tourists in central Havana are fakes; it should be stated plainly that this is a qualitative assessment based on reporting on the ground, not a figure backed by anything like government statistics. The conclusion, and the unanimous advice of the travel guides: buying a box from street or unofficial sellers amounts, in effect, to 'buying a fake knowingly'." },
    { p: "Price, the strongest signal of all",
      h: "A 'cheap Cohiba' cannot in principle exist. Official retail prices are high to begin with, and Cuba's official distribution network is structured not to sell at deep discounts, so a price far adrift of the market is itself strong evidence of counterfeiting. In one reported industry example, goods under the same brand name as a box selling in official shops in the high 300-dollar range were offered on the street at a fraction of that price (an individual case; the gap varies with circumstances). Rock-bottom discounts, the 'special price just for you' patter, online shops of unknown provenance and private deals over social media are all high-risk; the iron rule is to grow suspicious the moment a price feels 'too good to be true'." },
    { p: "Where the fakes are made — Habanos S.A.'s view and the controversy",
      h: "Habanos S.A. figures are reported to take the view that most counterfeit Habanos in world circulation are made not inside Cuba but abroad — Panama, the Dominican Republic, Honduras — and that the packaging itself, bands and boxes, is in many cases also produced abroad. The claim is contested, however: some in the industry point out that counterfeiting and diversion within Cuba (particularly through Havana's distribution channels) continue unabated. In 2024 a seizure at Beirut international airport of counterfeit Habanos believed to have originated in the Dominican Republic and travelled via Paris was reported — a glimpse of a supply network for fake cigars that has become multinational and international. The simple formula 'billed as Cuban-made, therefore safe' does not hold; it is the reliability of the whole distribution chain that needs examining." },
    { p: "Seizure statistics and estimates of market scale — how to handle the numbers",
      h: "As reported by Cigar Aficionado from Habanos S.A.'s account, Cuban customs seize on the order of some 700,000 cigars a year as counterfeit (the figure varies by year, and note that it rests on reported comments by company representatives, not on full disclosure of official statistics). The same article went on to present a calculation that even if only half the travellers visiting Cuba carried fakes home as souvenirs, the total would exceed 18 million cigars a year — but this is an estimate built on an assumption the reporters themselves supplied, not a measured value. In recent years too, Cuba's official and industry media have repeatedly reported seizures at Havana's José Martí international airport of counterfeit cigars on the scale of thousands, along with fake warranty stamps and counterfeiting materials, suggesting the problem is not past but ongoing. On the American side as well, enforcement cases targeting counterfeit cigars and complete sets of fake labels and packaging have been reported: counterfeiting is a structural problem straddling producing and consuming countries alike. Every figure cited here is an estimate resting on reporting and testimony, not independently audited official statistics, and should be treated as indicative on that understanding." },
    { p: "Legitimate channels — La Casa del Habano and the official distributors",
      h: "The surest defence against fakes is to buy only through channels whose authenticity is guaranteed in the first place. Habanos S.A.'s official directly franchised network, La Casa del Habano (LCDH), runs more than 140 shops across five continents, known as specialists that keep their stock in walk-in humidors under proper temperature and humidity control. The list of shops can be checked on the official Habanos site (habanos.com) and the official LCDH site, and each country's official import distributors are listed in the 'Distribuidores' section of the official site. Airport duty-free shops, unlicensed stalls and general stores, and online shops of unverifiable provenance carry a residual risk of imitations even when seals and holograms resembling the genuine article are attached — so verifying the legitimacy of the seller itself before buying is a more effective defence than any amount of physical examination of individual boxes." },
    { p: "Consistency in tubes and cellophane",
      h: "The quality of individual packaging — aluminium tubes (tubos) and cellophane wrapping — also holds clues to authenticity. Genuine tubes are precisely printed and finished, with caps that seat positively; the cigar inside tube or cellophane matches the size correctly, without an unnaturally large gap. Coarse printing on tube or film, blurred logos, loose caps, contents so slim they swim about — such inconsistencies are grounds for caution. Packaging specifications change with marque and vintage, however, and genuine articles vary too, so this also is best kept as a supporting indicator, read together with other features rather than alone." },
    { p: "Traces of added or swapped bands and seals",
      h: "A stock method of forgery, as reported, is to dress cheap cigars after the fact in official-looking bands or seals, or to refill a genuine box with something else. Observers such as the Holt's firm note that on fake Cohiba boxes the warranty seal is frequently not glued to the box itself but merely tucked in on adhesive paper — a sloppy treatment said to be an almost consistent mark of the fake. A seal or band unnaturally new against box or cigar, out of position, or with glue traces exposed — anything giving the impression of having been 'attached later' — is a strong warning sign. Conversely, any trace of the self-destructing seal having once been peeled is grounds to suspect the contents have been switched." },
    { p: "Judging online purchases and lots of unknown provenance",
      h: "Online sellers without physical premises, and private deals through social media or message boards, are channels with little assurance of legitimacy and a comparatively high risk of counterfeits and switching. Improbably abundant stock, every size always ready for immediate dispatch, prices a notch below the market, payment confined to irreversible methods, a seller whose substance and whereabouts cannot be verified — the more of these signs coincide, the higher the danger. The established practice in enthusiast communities is to check a seller's reputation and record in third-party venues (forums and reviews) before buying, and to pass at the slightest doubt. The more loudly a pitch stresses cheapness or rarity, the more coolly the provenance demands to be examined." },
    { p: "Sensory signs in the draw and burn behaviour",
      h: "How a cigar behaves once actually lit is also a telling clue, if an after-the-fact one. A genuine, well-rolled Cuban draws with moderate resistance, gives generous volumes of smoke, holds a straight, even burn line (the advancing edge of the fire), and tends to form ash that holds together at a consistent length and resists falling. A counterfeit carelessly stuffed with scrap leaf is prone to faults: a plugged, extremely heavy draw, or the opposite — loose, hot and harsh; a burn line running askew (tunnelling or canoeing); a fire that keeps going out; an acrid, half-cooked smell. Genuine cigars can show the same symptoms from their storage conditions (over-dry or over-humid), however, so this too should remain an after-the-fact indicator, weighed with the outward features in a rounded judgement." },
    { p: "The principle of combined judgement — never decide on a single piece of evidence",
      h: "None of the points raised so far — seal, hologram, barcode, factory code, wording, band printing, quality of the roll, box construction, packaging, price, purchase channel — is certain proof on its own. Sophisticated counterfeits imitate even seals and holograms, while genuine articles, through wear in distribution or differences of vintage, can depart from the 'textbook' in places. The basis of authentication is the idea of multiple verification — layering several independent clues to raise confidence — and the conclusion widely shared across the industry press and enthusiast communities is that a single strong incongruity, above all in price and purchase channel, should counsel caution even when every other feature looks genuine." }
  ];
  if (A.spottingFakes.length === SPF.length) A.spottingFakes.forEach(function (o, i) { Object.assign(o, SPF[i]); });

  A.ccVsNc = "【Defining CC and NC】Enthusiasts abbreviate: 'CC = Cuban Cigar (a Cuban Habano)', 'NC = Non-Cuban'. The main NC origins are Nicaragua, the Dominican Republic and Honduras, and the relative merits of the two are the cigar world's eternal debate.\n【The embargo as starting point】In 1962 the United States embargoed Cuban cigars. At almost the same time, the nationalisation of industry in 1961 sent most of the old brand owners into exile, where they founded same-named brands or new marques in other countries — the origin of today's double structure.\n【Trademarks in duplicate】As a result, Cohiba, Montecristo, Partagás and others exist in parallel as Cuban versions (Habanos) and non-Cuban versions (separate companies). The same names circulating within the US are almost all the non-Cuban versions: the name is shared, but leaf and blend are entirely different things.\n【The Cohiba trademark suit】The greatest dispute concerns Cohiba: in 1997 Cubatabaco sued General Cigar (now under STG) over the Cohiba trademark within the US. After a quarter-century of battle, it was reported that a federal district court (Judge Brinkema) upheld the 2022 TTAB decision, allowing the cancellation of General's marks. The litigation nevertheless has a long history of continued contest.\n【The Montecristo story】Montecristo too, under the effects of trademark disputes, saw manufacture of the non-Cuban version in the Canary Islands cease and production move to La Romana in the Dominican Republic. The tug-of-war over the great names continues, marque by marque, to this day.\n【Taste I: the Cuban twang】The core of the CC is the singular 'Cuban twang' born of Vuelta Abajo's mineral-rich soils — earth, hay, honey and wood, layered with the complex savour that fermentation brings. In well-aged examples, strength and smoothness living side by side is the signature.\n【Taste II: character by origin】NC character divides by origin: Nicaragua is powerful and spicy, with cacao and pepper; the Dominican Republic creamy and mild; Honduras earthy, sweet, at times floral. A fine illustration of terroir — the character of the land — deciding flavour.\n【The quality debate】'Cuba supreme' was once the received wisdom, but in recent years unevenness in Cuban quality and rolling has been pointed out, while the NC summit (Padrón, Arturo Fuente OpusX, Davidoff and others) being judged equal or better in blind tastings has become a commonplace.\n【The connoisseur's conclusion】The received view now is that 'CC and NC are not ranks but different genres'. Cuba offers a fermentation aroma and terroir like nothing else; NC offers consistent execution and a wealth of blends — a division of roles, and the view that knowing both broadens one's understanding of cigars has taken firm root.\n【The embargo in practice】US residents remain, as a rule, restricted from buying and importing Cuban cigars. What stands on American shelves as 'Cohiba' or 'Montecristo' is the non-Cuban version, and travellers bringing cigars home can also fall within the regulations — care is needed, since the same name can mean different contents and different legal treatment.\n【Two philosophies of blending】Cuba builds its blends, as a rule, from its own leaf alone — a single-origin construction. NC houses combine multinational leaf from Nicaragua, the Dominican Republic, Ecuador, Mexico and elsewhere, with great freedom to design a flavour to order; this structural difference underlies the differences in character and consistency of taste.\n【Ageing and the axis of time】Many CCs are held to be rough when young, shedding their edges over a few years' ageing before showing their true worth, while NCs are often praised as polished on release and ready for immediate service. The pleasure of waiting against satisfaction now — this difference of time-scale is another face of the division of roles.\n【The asymmetry of access】In most of the world both CC and NC can be bought; in the US the CC is as a rule unobtainable, while in Cuba itself the NC summit is hard to encounter. The longing each camp feels for the side it cannot have is part of the psychology that keeps the debate at the boil.\n【The lesson of blind tasting】In blind tastings, with marques and bands hidden, assumptions fail surprisingly often. The cigar praised to the skies in the belief it was a CC that turned out to be an NC is a stock cautionary tale shared among enthusiasts — a reminder of the importance of setting preconceptions aside and judging with one's own palate.";

  var LEG = [
    { name: "Cohiba Behike BHK 56",
      why: "The largest gauge of 'Behike', the summit Cohiba line unveiled at the 12th Habanos Festival in 2010. The first blend to use the rare medio tiempo leaf (small, intense leaves that grow only at the very top of some plants), it yields dense, concentrated layers of sweetness, spice and coffee. Made in tiny quantities at El Laguito, it is held to stand at the peak in both price and rarity (Cigar Aficionado's 2010 Cigar of the Year, meanwhile, was the same line's BHK 52, at 97 points)." },
    { name: "Montecristo No.2 (Pirámide)",
      why: "The 'king of torpedoes', virtually unchanged since 1935. Its galera is the Piramide (156×52); the tapered head concentrates the smoke, and the poise of leather, sweet spice (cinnamon, nutmeg) and wood stands out. In 2013 it took Cigar Aficionado's blind-judged No.1 of the year with 96 points, the marque's highest score to that date. Far more affordable than Behike yet first-class in taste — the emblem of value for money." },
    { name: "Partagás Serie D No.4",
      why: "The textbook robusto. Born in the 1930s as one of the A–D series for the British market, discontinued in the 1960s, then revived in 1975 on the rising popularity of the robusto. With its powerful, classically Cuban flavours of leather, earth, pepper and espresso and its dependable rolling, it is loved as 'the benchmark of the rich Cuban cigar' — a staple of staples. Partagás, founded in 1845, is the standard-bearer of the powerful, earthy style. The Serie D No.4 unites availability, price and taste; it is recommended as a first serious Cuban robusto, yet remains — rarely for a cigar — the 'place to come home to' that veterans return to daily." },
    { name: "Trinidad Fundadores",
      why: "The 'phantom diplomatic cigar', rolled from 1969 and, from 1980, produced in strict secrecy solely for Castro and for diplomatic gifts. Its galera is the Laguito Especial (192×40), slender and elegant, with the pigtail as its marker. Public sale began in 1998; its refined taste of honey, cedar and delicate spice, and its origin at El Laguito, Cohiba's own home, give it a high mythology." },
    { name: "Cohiba Lanceros",
      why: "Laguito No.1 (192×38). The original Cohiba size, created in the 1960s by Castro's personal roller — the very starting point of the Cohiba brand itself. Long and slender, demanding the height of rolling skill to keep even, it remains an object of the devotee's longing for its delicate, aromatic taste and its historical weight. In the narrow gauge the changes of ageing show themselves with great delicacy — the appeal of watching youthful greenness open, over a few years, into blossoming aroma. And because at this size the quality of the roll bears directly on the draw, the satisfaction of striking a good example is held to be something apart." },
    { name: "Bolívar Royal Coronas",
      why: "The robusto that took 94 points and Cigar Aficionado's No.1 of the year in 2006. A full body of Cuba's strongest class — earth, dark chocolate, black pepper and leather in waves — held to shed its edges and reveal its true worth only with long ageing: a cigar for the seasoned smoker. Bolívar, named after the South American liberator Simón Bolívar, is the marque of the weighty style, and among its cigars the Royal Coronas is the emblem of power. Fierce almost to roughness when young, it is told as the classic case of a cigar whose reputation transforms once a few years' rest opens its sweetness and complexity." },
    { name: "Cohiba Esplendidos",
      why: "A Julieta No.2 (178×47 — the Churchill shape) introduced in 1989: Cohiba's flagship and one of the most famous Cuban cigars in the world. With its refined sweet aromas of honey, cedar and roasted nuts, and its balance, it holds a place apart as the staple of gifts and celebrations. The skill of rolling 178mm evenly, joined to the mellowness of leaf put through Cohiba's distinctive additional fermentation, makes it counted among the summits of the Cuban cigar — a symbolic presence with no shortage of anecdotes of famous devotees." },
    { name: "Ramón Allones Specially Selected",
      why: "A robusto (124×50). Using the weighty leaf of the Partagás school, yet with the sweetness and roundness of earth, cacao and dried fruit, it is sometimes called 'the cigar the connoisseur arrives at last'. Long recommended among enthusiasts as a slightly gentler alternative to the Serie D No.4 — a staple robusto. Ramón Allones, founded in 1837, is an old-guard marque with a settled reputation for its rich box aroma (owing to the quality of the leaf, not to box-pressing) and for the dense sweetness that opens with ageing; its standing among expert smokers is particularly high." },
    { name: "Hoyo de Monterrey Epicure No.2",
      why: "A Hermoso No.4 (127×48). A gentle, refined medium body of cedar, almond and light leather — the flagship of Hoyo de Monterrey. Its quality without excess of strength has kept it for years the representative 'daytime Cuban cigar', a fine piece that serves beginner and connoisseur alike. Hoyo de Monterrey is known for a delicate, elegant style, and in a modern age inclined to favour full body it shows the appeal of another axis: lightness with complexity. The Epicure No.2 is held to embody that philosophy most plainly." },
    { name: "Cohiba Siglo VI",
      why: "A Cañonazo (150×52), the summit size of the Línea 1492. Combining concentrated honey, cedar and roasted notes with Cohiba's characteristic smoothness, this thickish robusto extra earned worldwide acclaim as the brand's finest performer before the arrival of Behike. Since its debut in 2002 it has enjoyed immense popularity as the grand Cohiba for gifts, trading high on the secondary market. Thick of gauge yet refined in taste, it is a cigar that marks Cohiba's modern high-water mark." }
  ];
  if (A.legendaryCuban.length === LEG.length) A.legendaryCuban.forEach(function (o, i) { Object.assign(o, LEG[i]); });

  A.cotyConcept = "【What COTY is】'Cigar of the Year' (COTY) denotes the No.1 spot in the 'Top 25', the annual ranking published at each year's end since 2004 by the authoritative American magazine Cigar Aficionado — the most influential honour in the cigar world. From the moment of selection prices surge and stocks vanish; the market impact is such that for a maker, a single award can change the fate of the company. Since its founding in 1992 the magazine has rooted in America a culture of 'talking about cigars as one talks about wine', raising blind tasting and the 100-point scale to industry standards. The Top 25 and its No.1 stand on that accumulation as the year's grand reckoning.\n\n【The 100-point scoring system】The magazine scores cigars out of 100, as with wine and spirits. Marks are allotted chiefly across four aspects — appearance/construction, burn/draw, flavour and overall impression — and summed into a score. The bands are graded: 95–100 = 'Classic' (a masterpiece), 90–94 = 'Outstanding', 80–89 = 'Very good to excellent', 70–79 = 'Average to good commercial quality', and 0–69 = 'Don't waste your money'. The high nineties, and above all a 'Classic' at 95 or over, is the mark of the pantheon, reached by only a small fraction of cigars in the magazine's history.\n\n【A rigorously blind method】All scoring is done blind. A dedicated tasting coordinator buys the cigars from trusted retail tobacconists in the ordinary market, exactly as a member of the public would, then strips the bands so that brand, origin and factory cannot be known, replacing them with plain, numbered labels. The editors and tasters score without knowing the marque, so a brand's aura and preconceptions are kept from swaying the numbers. This thoroughness about fairness is the wellspring of the magazine's authority.\n\n【How the Top 25 and the No.1 are decided】Cigars that score highly in the year's ordinary tastings (roughly 91 points and above) become candidates for the year-end selection, and after several rounds of blind re-tasting the 'Top 25' and its summit — the No.1 — are settled. In the final selection, availability, price, buzz and a completeness that captures the year are weighed alongside the score. The highest score is therefore not automatically No.1: from closely matched cigars, the one fittest to be 'the face of the year' is chosen.\n\n【Tendencies across the years】The advance of the New World is striking, and Nicaragua is the country that has produced the most No.1s. By brand, Padrón leads all others with five wins — 2004, 2007, 2009, 2021 and 2025, the most in the industry — the 2025 summit going to the Padrón 60th Anniversary (97 points). Recent No.1s include: 2010, Cohiba Behike BHK 52 (97 points, the highest recent rating for a Cuban); 2011, Alec Bradley Prensado; 2012, Flor de las Antillas (My Father); 2013, Montecristo No.2; 2014, Oliva Serie V Melanio; 2015, My Father Le Bijou 1922; 2016, La Flor Dominicana Andalusian Bull; 2018, E.P. Carrillo Encore Majestic; 2020, E.P. Carrillo Pledge (98 points); 2022, H. Upmann No.2; 2023, Fuente Fuente OpusX Reserva d'Chateau (97 points); 2024, My Father The Judge; and 2025, Padrón 60th Anniversary.\n\n【The debate over 100 and 99】The highest score ever given in the history of the blind tastings is 99, and it went to one cigar only: the Hoyo de Monterrey Double Corona in the winter 1992/93 issue. That 99 has never been matched since, and it is spoken of as a legendary benchmark. A perfect 100 has appeared on very rare occasions in 'Connoisseur's Corner', a special feature that is not blind, but in the regular blind tastings it remains ground no one has reached. The weight of the scores and the subjectivity of the Top 25 selection stir lively argument among enthusiasts every year — which itself speaks for the award's cultural presence.\n\n【The market effects of the award】The announcement of the No.1 often turns an already-released cigar scarce and expensive overnight. What could be had at list price commonly trades at several times that once the award lands, and makers sometimes anticipate it with increased production or re-releases. On the other hand, the examples bought blind and those hurried to market after the award can differ in state of ageing, and experienced buyers try to establish whether a box is from 'the awarded lot itself'. The award is a great mover of buying behaviour, and the year-end Top 25 announcement has become a seasonal fixture of the cigar world.\n\n【The tug-of-war between Cuba and the New World】Cigar Aficionado is an American publication, and its subject matter centres on the non-Cuban cigars legally distributed within the US. The structure therefore favours New World production — Nicaragua, the Dominican Republic, Honduras — at the top of the table; yet Cuban cigars (Cohiba, Montecristo, H. Upmann and others), tasted through a separate track, have repeatedly taken the No.1 spot, so merit is judged across the divide of origin. Following the roll of past winners, the qualitative advance of the New World since the 2000s — of Nicaragua above all — can be read clearly in the numbers.";

  var HSC = [
    { name: "Hoyo de Monterrey Double Corona (Cuba)",
      note: "99 points in the blind tasting of the winter 1992/93 issue — the highest score Cigar Aficionado has ever awarded under blind conditions, an imperishable benchmark unmatched after more than thirty years. A cigar marking the summit of the mild, graceful Cuban classic." },
    { name: "E.P. Carrillo Pledge (Pledge Prequel)",
      note: "Cigar of the Year 2020, at 98 points — a score of the highest modern class, second only to the sole blind 99 (the Hoyo Double Corona). The crystallisation of refinement and power from the master Ernesto Pérez-Carrillo, showing what Dominican production can reach." },
    { name: "Cohiba Behike BHK 52",
      note: "Cigar of the Year 2010 (97 points). From Cuba's summit line using the rare medio tiempo leaf — the most highly rated Cuban cigar of recent times. The honeyed creaminess born of the El Laguito factory is its crowning glory." },
    { name: "Padrón 1926 Serie No.9",
      note: "Cigar of the Year 2007 (97 points). A dense harmony of cacao, coffee and black pepper concentrated in the box-pressed form, hailed as a high-water mark of the New World cigar. Padrón's second No.1." },
    { name: "Fuente Fuente OpusX Reserva d'Chateau",
      note: "Cigar of the Year 2023 (97 points). The peak of the 100% Dominican puro legend with which Carlito Fuente overturned the impossible, uniting high scores with the cult popularity born of its distinctive rosado wrapper and its scarcity." },
    { name: "My Father Le Bijou 1922 Torpedo Box Pressed",
      note: "Cigar of the Year 2015 (97 points). The representative masterpiece among Pepín García's spicy, dense Nicaraguan puros. Bearing his father's birth year, 1922, it reached the summit through exacting rolling and profound sweetness." },
    { name: "Padrón 60th Anniversary",
      note: "Cigar of the Year 2025 (97 points, the brand's fifth win). A round perfecto of 6 × 56, presented in a gold coffin-style case. A homage to founder José O. Padrón and to sixty years of history — the culmination that sealed the record for most No.1s of any brand." },
    { name: "Oliva Serie V Melanio Figurado",
      note: "Cigar of the Year 2014 (96 points). The weighty sweetness of Jalapa Valley ligero at a completeness out of all proportion to its price. Embodying 'high quality yet affordable', it became the emblem of value for money." },
    { name: "Montecristo No.2 (Cuba)",
      note: "Cigar of the Year 2013 (96 points). The tapering pirámide hailed as 'the greatest torpedo ever made' — a reference instrument of the Cuban cigar, perfecting the harmony of cacao and coffee." },
    { name: "Fuente Fuente OpusX Double Corona",
      note: "Cigar of the Year 2005 (95 points). The monument by which the OpusX legend — which overturned the received wisdom that a Dominican wrapper was impossible — was first acknowledged to the world as No.1." },
    { name: "Bolívar Royal Coronas (Cuba)",
      note: "Cigar of the Year 2006 (94 points). A full body of Cuba's strongest class — earth, dark chocolate and black pepper in waves — concentrated into a compact robusto. It shows its true worth only with long ageing." },
    { name: "My Father The Judge Grand Robusto",
      note: "Cigar of the Year 2024. A dense Nicaraguan showing Pepín García's craft at its sharpest, confirming once more the strength of the modern powerhouse that has brought My Father several No.1s." },
    { name: "E.P. Carrillo Encore Majestic",
      note: "Cigar of the Year 2018 (96 points). The cigar with which the master Ernesto Pérez-Carrillo first stood at the summit under his own brand — paired with the Pledge of 2020, two No.1s achieved within a short span." }
  ];
  if (A.legendaryHighscore.length === HSC.length) A.legendaryHighscore.forEach(function (o, i) { Object.assign(o, HSC[i]); });
})();
